  "headline": "Yields through 5% and oil up 4%, yet gold falls: the Fed is still being believed",
  "body": [
    "Today the market started paying for the war and still sold gold. Brent jumped 4% to $95.75 while the 10-year broke through 5.0% to 5.10%, and stocks slipped 0.35% rather than cheering. That is the first session in weeks that fits my old script, oil lifting yields and leaning on equities, yet gold fell another 1.9% to $3,909, the floor of its yearly range, and the dollar at 103.2 printed a fresh high.",
    "Yesterday's cut held. My condition for buying gold back was a close above $4,000 on a day the dollar fell; today delivered the opposite on both counts. I said 5.0% on the 10-year was the round number stocks were living with, and they lived with it for one day. The verdict stands: the thesis is broken on gold and the dollar, and today only adds a crack in the other direction on oil and yields.",
    "The curve tells me which signal to trust. The front end led again, the 5-year up 12 basis points on the day against 8 on the 30-year, and the 30-year minus 5-year spread flattened to 49 basis points. That is a policy repricing, oil read as a reason for the Fed to hold or hike, and gold falling as yields rise is the signature of rising real rates, not of a Treasury financed by accommodation. A MOVE at 98, up 6% today and in the top third of its range, says the bond market is uneasy, not breaking, so I take the market's side over my own for now.",
    "Drop Site says the US is rushing military assets to the Middle East as negotiations falter, MintPress calls the latest exchange of strikes a dramatic escalation, and Electronic Intifada flags the Strait of Hormuz in its latest livestream. Brent is finally answering, but 4% on the day still leaves it down 10% on the month, and one session is not an energy leg. The yen is the tell I believe, USD/JPY up 0.65% to 159.7, the importer paying first, while the rupee keeps firming with USD/INR down 2.2% on the month. Bitcoin is no hedge in any of this: down 4.6% today and down 10% against gold over the month, it is a risk asset being drained of liquidity.",
    "My book: I stay out of gold at $3,909 and the condition for returning is unchanged. I stay out of the 30-year at 5.43%, because if the Fed is believed the pain lands on the front end and the long bond merely follows. I would own Brent rather than gold as the war hedge, since today showed which one the market pays on escalation. I keep short USD/INR but tighten it: if Brent holds above $95 and the rupee stops firming, I cover, because the rupee is the first casualty of a real oil leg. What brings the old view back is the 30-year minus 5-year spread widening through 60 basis points as stocks fall. Next I watch Brent at $100."
  ],
